# How to redirect Laravel route from Javascript file in blade template

Attempting to trigger a full-page navigation after an AJAX success handler is a common pattern, but the implementation details often trip up developers who aren't careful with scope and rendering contexts. The error you encounteredâ`Route [/show-all-prescription] not defined`âisn't about whether your route exists in `routes/web.php`. It's about **where** the string containing that route name is being evaluated.

In your specific case, the issue stems from mixing server-side Blade directives with client-side JavaScript execution. You attempted to place `{{ route('/show-all-prescription')}}` directly inside the AJAX success callback. When the browser executes that line of JavaScript, it isn't running PHP code; it's running raw text strings found in the HTML source.

Because the Blade engine processes the file before sending any data to the browser, the `{{ }}` syntax is never interpreted at runtime within the JSON response or the DOM. The browser simply sees the literal text `{% route('/show-all-prescription') %}` (or whatever was rendered based on your specific version quirks), resulting in a broken JavaScript string that fails to match any valid URL structure, leading to routing errors or silent failures depending on how the error is handled.

To fix this, you must ensure the destination URL is resolved *once* during the initial page load and stored as a constant value within the `<script>` tag, rather than trying to resolve it again inside the asynchronous callback.

### Resolving Routes in Blade Templates

The correct approach is to define your route names or URLs at the top of your Blade template, before any JavaScript logic is executed. You can assign these to variables that are then available to your jQuery handlers.

Here is how you should structure your Blade file:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>Prescription Form</title>
    <!-- Ensure jQuery is loaded before your custom scripts -->
    <script src="https://code.jquery.com/jquery-3.6.0.min.js"></script>
</head>
<body>

<form id="prescriptionForm">
    <input type="text" name="name" placeholder="Name">
    <input type="number" name="age" placeholder="Age">
    <input type="text" name="mobile_no" placeholder="Mobile No">
    <button type="submit">Submit</button>
</form>

<script>
    // 1. Resolve the route here, while Blade is still active on the server side
    const targetRouteUrl = "{{ url('/show-all-prescription') }}";

    // Alternatively, if you prefer named routes:
    // const targetRouteName = "show.all.prescription"; 
    // Note: url() is generally safer for redirects unless using named routes strictly

    $('#prescriptionForm').on('submit', function(e) {
        e.preventDefault();

        var formData = $(this).serialize();

        $.ajax({
            url: "{{ url('/submit_prescription') }}",
            type: 'POST', // Changed from GET to POST for form data
            data: formData,
            success: function(msg) {
                if (msg > 0) {
                    // 2. Redirect using the pre-resolved variable
                    window.location.href = targetRouteUrl;
                }
            },
            error: function(xhr) {
                console.error("Submission failed:", xhr);
            }
        });
    });
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

### Why This Works

By moving the `url()` call into the `<script>` block at the top level, you leverage the Blade engine's ability to render variables before the HTML is sent to the client. The browser receives a clean JavaScript file where `targetRouteUrl` holds the actual string (e.g., `http://localhost/show-all-prescription`).

When your AJAX callback executes, it accesses this constant variable. This eliminates the dependency on PHP syntax inside the JavaScript runtime and prevents the "Route not defined" error you saw earlier.

### Important Considerations

**HTTP Methods Matter**
In your source material example, the AJAX request used `type: 'GET'` to submit form data. While technically possible, sending large amounts of data via GET parameters has limitations (URL length constraints) and exposes sensitive data in browser history. Laravel best practices suggest using `POST` for form submissions. Ensure your route definition in `routes/web.php` accepts POST requests if you change the AJAX type:

```php
Route::post('/submit_prescription', [PrescriptionController::class, 'store']);
Route::get('/show-all-prescription', [PrescriptionController::class, 'show_all_prescription']);
```

**SPA vs. Traditional Routing**
If your application is a Single Page Application (SPA) where the server doesn't handle routing for every page load, relying on `window.location.href` might reload the entire application, losing state or causing re-initialization issues. In such scenarios, you would typically trigger an internal navigation method provided by your frontend framework (like `router.push()` in Vue.js or `history.pushState` in React) instead of a full browser redirect.

For traditional Laravel applications served via Apache/Nginx, however, the server-side route handling takes over immediately after `window.location.href` is called, making the pre-resolved Blade variable the most robust solution.
